<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suyab</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suyab was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> city in the Chu valley of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, at the site of Ak-Beshim near Tokmok. It served as a seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, where the pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> met Tong Yabghu around 630, and later of the Turgesh. In 679 the Tang built a fortress there as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-garrisons-of-anxi">Four Garrisons of Anxi</a>. Its ruins are part of the Silk Roads World Heritage listing.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suyab, also called Ordukent, lay in the Chu river valley some 50 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishkek">Bishkek</a> and 8 kilometres west-south-west of Tokmok, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>. A settlement of Sogdian merchants grew up there along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> in the 5th or 6th century. Its name comes from the Suyab River, an Iranian name meaning toward the water. The Chinese pilgrim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who passed in 629, described a walled city where merchants from many nations gathered, with a cold, windy climate and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and grapes grown in the soil.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under Tong Yabghu Qaghan, Suyab was the principal capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a>, which also had a summer capital at Navekat. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> provided economic prosperity and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> military security. After the khaganate fell, Suyab was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang dynasty</a> and served as a military outpost between 648 and 719. A Chinese fortress was built in 679 by Wang Fangyi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> flourished. According to some accounts the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-bai">Li Bai</a> was born there. The Old Book of Tang says the city had twelve gates on four sides, curved to hide its inhabitants.</p><p>Suyab was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-garrisons-of-anxi">Four Garrisons of Anxi</a> until 719, when it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suluk">Suluk</a> of the Türgesh, whom the Tang court named Loyal and Obedient Qaghan. After Suluk was murdered in 738, Tang forces retook it, and it was strategically important in the wars against the Tibetan Empire. The traveller Du Huan found a working Buddhist monastery among its ruins in 751. In 766 it fell to a Karluk ruler allied with the nascent Uyghur Khaganate, and little is recorded after the Chinese left the Four Garrisons in 787. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudud-al-alam">Hudud al-Alam</a>, completed in 983, lists it as a city of 20,000 inhabitants. It is believed to have been supplanted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a> in the early 11th century and abandoned soon after.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In the 19th century the ruins at Ak-Beshim were wrongly identified with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balasagun">Balasagun</a>, the capital of the Kara-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khitans">Khitans</a>, and Vasily Bartold supported this after visiting in 1893–1894. Excavations began in 1938, and in the 1950s it was shown that the site had been abandoned in the 11th century, while Balasagun flourished until the 14th. The site covers some 30 hectares and holds Chinese fortifications, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christian</a> churches, Zoroastrian ossuaries and Turkic balbals, with many Buddha statues and stelae. There was a 7th-century Nestorian church and cemetery, and probably a 10th-century monastery with frescoes and inscriptions in Sogdian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> scripts. The ruins were inscribed in 2014 as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Roads</a>: the Routes Network of Chang'an-Tianshan Corridor World Heritage Site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suyab” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Led by a yabghu, the Karluks took <b>Suyab</b> and the lands of the Turgesh Khaganate by 766, ruling a mix of nomadic tribes and the Sogdian towns of the Chu valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/karluk-yabgu">Karluk Yabgu</a></li><li><blockquote>Their chief Wuzhile took <b>Suyab</b> around 699 as the Western Turkic state collapsed, and his son Saqal succeeded him before being killed by the Second Turkic Khaganate in 711.</blockquote><a href="/state/turgesh-khaganate">Turgesh Khaganate</a></li><li><blockquote>The khagans ruled from summer pastures and courts such as <b>Suyab</b> in the Chu valley over the 'On Oq', ten arrows or tribes in two wings.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a></li></ul></section>
